  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "School vocabulary is full of commands. Teachers speak to the whole class, so you’ll hear plural commands all day.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Musome ekitabo.", "base": "Read the book, everyone." },
      { "target": "Muwulirize omusomesa.", "base": "Listen to the teacher, everyone." },
      { "target": "Nnina ekibuuzo.", "base": "I have a question." },
      { "target": "Otegeera?", "base": "Do you understand?" },
      { "target": "Ddamu mpola, ssebo.", "base": "Repeat slowly, sir." },
      { "target": "Nnyagala nnyo okubala.", "base": "I really like maths." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "class-commands",
        "title": "Commands to a group",
        "body": [
          "To one person, a command is the bare verb: **Soma** (read), **Wandiika** (write). To a group, add **mu-** in front and change the final **-a** to **-e**: **Musome**, **Muwandiike**, **Muwulirize**.",
          "The same **-e** ending makes “let’s”: **Tusome** (let’s read). You’ll meet it again in **Tugende** (let’s go)."
        ],
        "table": [
          ["One person", "Everyone", "Let’s"],
          ["Soma", "Musome", "Tusome"],
          ["Wandiika", "Muwandiike", "Tuwandiike"],
          ["Wuliriza", "Muwulirize", "Tuwulirize"]
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "n-sound-changes",
        "title": "n- changes the sound after it",
        "body": [
          "When **n-** (I) meets **w**, it becomes **mp**: **wandiika** gives **mpandiika** (I write). With **l** or **r** it becomes **nd**: **laba** gives **ndaba** (I see). You have seen this in **mpulira** and **mpa** too."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Languages at school",
        "body": [
          "Uganda has over forty languages. Schools teach in English, and many in central Uganda also teach Luganda. It is common to grow up speaking three languages."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
